WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:06.189
We have a voicemail number two one nine two
David p with a very interesting question about

00:00:06.189 --> 00:00:08.170
improving the democratic debates.

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:09.510
Take a listen to this.

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:15.850
Hi, David was the last Democratic presidential
candidate debate being largely obsessed on

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:18.689
the substantial and showing candidates, positions
and policy.

00:00:18.689 --> 00:00:25.320
Aside from the obvious need to reduce participants,
what would you suggest change and the next

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:26.539
debate to improve this?

00:00:26.539 --> 00:00:28.679
Maybe there could be better questions asked.

00:00:28.679 --> 00:00:29.679
Thanks.

00:00:29.679 --> 00:00:30.679
Yeah.

00:00:30.679 --> 00:00:32.949
So the, I mean, there's so much that needs
to happen to really improve the quality of

00:00:32.949 --> 00:00:33.949
these debates.

00:00:33.949 --> 00:00:35.649
Number one, we do need fewer candidates.

00:00:35.649 --> 00:00:37.440
That's going to happen by the third debate.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:38.879
So that is going to take care of itself.

00:00:38.879 --> 00:00:44.120
Uh, I think that we need a narrow range of
topics so the debates should really be much

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:46.320
more highly focused.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:53.359
So that, uh, number one, you can go into more
depth on particular topics and you can actually

00:00:53.359 --> 00:00:58.239
follow up and moderators should be following
up with questions in order to really figure

00:00:58.239 --> 00:01:01.539
out who knows in depth about these issues.

00:01:01.539 --> 00:01:06.970
Because when you have a debate format where
there's 10 people on the stage, uh, not every

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:09.500
candidate even gets answer every question.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:14.270
The ones that do get only 60 seconds, only
some candidates get to issue a rebuttal.

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:19.420
And the ones that do only get 30 seconds,
it's too easy to skirt by without having to

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:22.079
actually demonstrate that you know, anything
about these issues.

00:01:22.079 --> 00:01:28.950
So I would drastically narrow the focus of
each debate, tax policy and inequality and

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:31.090
entire debate on that.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:35.490
If you have only five or six candidates on
stage, you can actually give them, you know,

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:40.900
several minutes for their initial response
and then give other candidates several minutes

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:46.479
to rebut them, structure it in a way so that
the moderators can participate and actually

00:01:46.479 --> 00:01:51.649
ask followup and probing questions without
being accused of, of partisanship or trying

00:01:51.649 --> 00:01:53.530
to help one candidate or the other.

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:58.719
Um, everybody should be able to comment on
every question and the topics should be drastically

00:01:58.719 --> 00:02:04.369
narrowed so that you have many questions about
one topic and none of this is possible if

00:02:04.369 --> 00:02:07.789
the number of participants isn't decreased,
which it will be soon.

00:02:07.789 --> 00:02:09.729
Now, will they, will they actually make these
changes?

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:15.560
Probably not because these are what I call,
um, uh, the, the debates are structured to

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:18.959
be sort of argument porn.

00:02:18.959 --> 00:02:20.900
That's really the, the role of the moderators.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:22.819
And, uh, we really learned

00:02:22.819 --> 00:02:26.450
very little about the political positions
of our candidates when that's the format.

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:28.190
We've got a great bonus show for you today.

